The object has transform coefficients $\theta_{i}=\left\langle x, \psi_{i}\right\rangle$, and these are assumed sparse in the sense that, for some $0<p<2$ and for some $R>0$


\begin{equation*}
\|\theta\|_{p} \equiv\left(\sum_{i}\left|\theta_{i}\right|^{p}\right)^{1 / p} \leq R . \tag{I.1}
\end{equation*}

Indeed, we have trivially that, if $\theta_{N}$ denotes the vector $\theta$ with everything except the $N$ largest coefficients set to 0


\begin{equation*}
\left\|\theta-\theta_{N}\right\|_{2} \leq \zeta_{2, p} \cdot\|\theta\|_{p} \cdot(N+1)^{1 / 2-1 / p} \tag{I.2}
\end{equation*}


for $N=0,1,2, \ldots$, with a constant $\zeta_{2, p}$ depending only on $p \in(0,2)$.

Theorem 1: Let $\left(n, m_{n}\right)$ be a sequence of problem sizes with $n<m_{n}, n \rightarrow \infty$, and $m_{n} \sim A n^{\gamma}, \gamma>1, A>0$. Then for $0<p \leq 1$ there is $C_{p}=C_{p}(A, \gamma)>0$ so that


\begin{equation*}
E_{n}\left(X_{p, m}(R)\right) \leq C_{p} \cdot R \cdot\left(n / \log \left(m_{n}\right)\right)^{1 / 2-1 / p} . \tag{I.3}
\end{equation*}

Theorem 3: For $0<p \leq 1$ and $R>0$


\begin{align*}
d^{n}\left(X_{p, m}(R)\right) & \leq E_{n}\left(X_{p, m}(R)\right)  \tag{I.4}\\
& \leq 2^{1 / p-1} \cdot d^{n}\left(X_{p, m}(R)\right) . \tag{I.5}
\end{align*}

Then [8]


\begin{equation*}
d_{n}\left(b_{p, m} ; \ell_{q}\right)=d^{n}\left(b_{q^{\prime}, m}, \ell_{p^{\prime}}\right) \tag{I.6}
\end{equation*}

We define the width of a set $X$ relative to an operator $\Phi$


\begin{equation*}
w(\Phi, X) \equiv \sup \|x\|_{2} \text { subject to } x \in X \cap \operatorname{ker}(\Phi) . \tag{II.1}
\end{equation*}

Proof: Consider the optimization problem

$$
\left(Q_{p}\right) \quad \sup \|\theta\|_{2} \text { subject to } \Phi \theta=0, \quad\|\theta\|_{p} \leq 1 .
$$

Our goal is to bound the value of $\left(Q_{p}\right)$

$$
\operatorname{val}\left(Q_{p}\right) \leq C \cdot(n / \log (m / n))^{1 / 2-1 / p} .
$$

Choose $\theta$ so that $0=\Phi \theta$. Let $J$ denote the indices of the $k= \lfloor\rho n / \log (m)\rfloor$ largest values in $\theta$. Without loss of generality, suppose coordinates are ordered so that $J$ comes first among the $m$ entries, and partition $\theta=\left[\theta_{J}, \theta_{J^{c}}\right]$. Clearly


\begin{equation*}
\left\|\theta_{J^{c}}\right\|_{p} \leq\|\theta\|_{p} \leq 1 \tag{II.2}
\end{equation*}


while, because each entry in $\theta_{J}$ is at least as big as any entry in $\theta_{J^{c}}$, (I.2) gives


\begin{equation*}
\left\|\theta_{J^{c}}\right\|_{2} \leq \zeta_{2, p} \cdot(k+1)^{1 / 2-1 / p} \tag{II.3}
\end{equation*}


A similar argument for $\ell_{1}$ approximation gives, in case $p<1$


\begin{equation*}
\left\|\theta_{J^{c}}\right\|_{1} \leq \zeta_{1, p} \cdot(k+1)^{1-1 / p} \tag{II.4}
\end{equation*}


Now $0=\Phi_{J} \theta_{J}+\Phi_{J J^{c}} \theta_{J}{ }^{c}$. Hence, with $v=\Phi_{J} \theta_{J}$, we have $-v=\Phi_{J^{c}} \theta_{J^{c}}$. As $v \in V_{J}$ and $|J|=k<\rho n / \log (m)$, we can invoke CS3, getting

$$
\left\|\theta_{J^{c}}\right\|_{1} \geq Q_{J^{c}}(-v) \geq \eta_{3} / \sqrt{\log (m / n)} \cdot\|v\|_{1} .
$$

On the other hand, again using $v \in V_{J}$ and $|J|=k< \rho n / \log (m)$, invoke CS2, getting

$$
\|v\|_{1} \geq \eta_{2} \cdot \sqrt{n} \cdot\|v\|_{2} .
$$

Combining these with the above

$$
\begin{aligned}
\|v\|_{2} & \leq\left(\eta_{2} \eta_{3}\right)^{-1} \cdot(\sqrt{\log (m / n)} / \sqrt{n}) \cdot\left\|\theta_{J^{c}}\right\|_{1} \\
& \leq c_{1} \cdot(n / \log (m))^{1 / 2-1 / p}
\end{aligned}
$$

with $c_{1}=\zeta_{1, p} \rho^{1-1 / p} / \eta_{2} \eta_{3}$. Recalling $|J|=k<\rho n / \log (m)$, and invoking CS1 we have

$$
\left\|\theta_{J}\right\|_{2} \leq\left\|\Phi_{J} \theta_{J}\right\|_{2} / \eta_{1}=\|v\|_{2} / \eta_{1}
$$

In short, with $c_{2}=c_{1} / \eta_{1}$

$$
\begin{aligned}
\|\theta\|_{2} \leq & \left\|\theta_{J}\right\|_{2}+\left\|\theta_{J^{c}}\right\|_{2} \\
\leq & c_{2} \cdot(n / \log (m))^{1 / 2-1 / p} \\
& +\zeta_{2, p} \cdot(\rho n / \log (m))^{1 / 2-1 / p}
\end{aligned}
$$

The theorem follows with

$$
C=\left(\zeta_{1, p} \rho^{1-1 / p} /\left(\eta_{1} \eta_{2} \eta_{3}\right)+\zeta_{2, p} \rho^{1 / 2-1 / p}\right)
$$

Lemma 3.1: Let $y_{n}=I_{n}\left(x_{0}\right)$ where $x_{0} \in X_{p, m}(R)$ and $I_{n}$ is an optimal information operator, and let $\hat{x}$ be any element of $\hat{X}_{p, R}\left(y_{n}\right)$. Then for $0<p \leq 1$


\begin{equation*}
\left\|x_{0}-\hat{x}\right\|_{2} \leq 2 \cdot E_{n}\left(X_{p, m}(R)\right) . \tag{III.1}
\end{equation*}

More generally, if the information operator $I_{n}$ is only nearoptimal, then the same argument gives


\begin{equation*}
\left\|x_{0}-\hat{x}\right\|_{2} \leq 2 \cdot E_{n}\left(I_{n}, X_{p, m}(R)\right) . \tag{III.2}
\end{equation*}

This can be formulated as a linear programming problem: let $A$ be the $n$ by $2 m$ matrix $\left[\begin{array}{ll}\Phi & -\Phi\end{array}\right]$. The linear program


\begin{equation*}
\min _{z} 1^{T} z \text { subject to } A z=y_{n}, \quad z \geq 0 \tag{LP}
\end{equation*}


has a solution $z^{*}$, say, a vector in $R^{2 m}$ which can be partitioned as $z^{*}=\left[u^{*} v^{*}\right]$; then $\theta^{*}=u^{*}-v^{*}$ solves $\left(P_{1}\right)$. The reconstruction $\hat{x}_{1, n}$ is $\Psi \theta^{*}$. This linear program is typically considered computationally tractable. In fact, this problem has been studied in the signal analysis literature under the name Basis Pursuit [26]; in that work, very large-scale underdetermined problems-e.g., with $n=8192$ and $m=262144$-were solved successfully using interior-point optimization methods.

Lemma 4.2: Let $\theta_{0}$ be a vector in $R^{m}$ and $1_{J} \theta_{0}$ be the corresponding mutilated vector with entries $\theta_{0}(i) 1_{J}(i)$. Suppose that

$$
\left\|\theta_{0}-1_{J} \theta_{0}\right\|_{1} \leq \epsilon
$$

where $\nu(\Phi, J) \leq \nu_{0}<1 / 2$. Consider the instance of $\left(P_{1}\right)$ defined by $y=\Phi \theta_{0}$; the solution $\hat{\theta}_{1}$ of this instance of $\left(P_{1}\right)$ obeys


\begin{equation*}
\left\|\theta_{0}-\hat{\theta}_{1}\right\|_{1} \leq \frac{2 \epsilon}{1-2 \nu_{0}} \tag{IV.2}
\end{equation*}

Weak $\ell_{p}$ constraints have the following key property: if $\theta_{N}$ denotes a mutilated version of the vector $\theta$ with all except the $N$ largest items set to zero, then the inequality


\begin{equation*}
\left\|\theta-\theta_{N}\right\|_{q} \leq \zeta_{q, p} \cdot R \cdot(N+1)^{1 / q-1 / p} \tag{V.1}
\end{equation*}


is valid for $p<1$ and $q=1,2$, with $R=\|\theta\|_{w \ell_{p}}$.

Consider the class $\mathcal{F}(B)$ of functions $f(t), t \in[0,1]$ which are restrictions to the unit interval of functions belonging to the Bump Algebra $\mathcal{B}$ [2], with bump norm $\|f\|_{\mathcal{B}} \leq B$. This was mentioned in the Introduction, which observed that the wavelet coefficients at level $j$ obey $\left\|\theta^{(j)}\right\|_{1} \leq C \cdot B \cdot 2^{-j}$ where $C$ depends only on the wavelet used. Here and later we use standard wavelet analysis notations as in [36], [37], [2].

We consider two ways of approximating functions in $f$. In the classic linear scheme, we fix a "finest scale" $j_{1}$ and measure the resumé coefficients $\beta_{j_{1}, k}=\left\langle f, \varphi_{j_{1}, k}\right\rangle$ where $\varphi_{j, k}= 2^{j / 2} \varphi\left(2^{j} t-k\right)$, with $\varphi$ a smooth function integrating to 1 . Think of these as point samples at scale $2^{-j_{1}}$ after applying an antialiasing filter. We reconstruct by $P_{j_{1}} f=\sum_{k} \beta_{j_{1}, k} \varphi_{j_{1}, k}$ giving an approximation error

$$
\left\|f-P_{j_{1}} f\right\|_{2} \leq C \cdot\|f\|_{\mathcal{B}} \cdot 2^{-j_{1} / 2}
$$

with $C$ depending only on the chosen wavelet. There are $N= 2^{j_{1}}$ coefficients $\left(\beta_{j_{1}, k}\right)_{k}$ associated with the unit interval, and so the approximation error obeys

$$
\left\|f-P_{j_{1}} f\right\|_{2} \leq C \cdot\|f\|_{\mathcal{B}} \cdot N^{-1 / 2}
$$

In the compressed sensing scheme, we need also wavelets $\psi_{j, k}=2^{j / 2} \psi\left(2^{j} x-k\right)$ where $\psi$ is an oscillating function with mean zero. We pick a coarsest scale $j_{0}=j_{1} / 3$. We measure the resumé coefficients $\beta_{j_{0}, k}$-there are $2^{j_{0}}$ of these-and then let $\theta \equiv\left(\theta_{\ell}\right)_{\ell=1}^{m}$ denote an enumeration of the detail wavelet coefficients $\left(\left(\alpha_{j, k}: 0 \leq k<2^{j}\right): j_{0} \leq j<j_{1}\right)$. The dimension $m$ of $\theta$ is $m=2^{j_{1}}-2^{j_{0}}$. The norm $\|\theta\|_{1}$ satisfies

$$
\|\theta\|_{1} \leq \sum_{j_{0}}^{j_{1}}\left\|\left(\alpha_{j, k}\right)\right\|_{1} \leq \sum_{j_{0}}^{j_{1}} C \cdot\|f\|_{\mathcal{B}} \cdot 2^{-j} \leq c B 2^{-j_{0}} .
$$

This establishes the constraint on $\ell_{1}$ norm needed for our theory. We take $n=c \cdot 2^{j_{0}} \log \left(2^{j_{1}}\right)$ and apply a near-optimal information operator for this $n$ and $m$ (described in more detail later). We apply the near-optimal algorithm of $\ell_{1}$ minimization, getting the error estimate

$$
\begin{aligned}
\|\hat{\theta}-\theta\|_{2} & \leq c\|\theta\|_{1} \cdot(n / \log (m))^{-1 / 2} \\
& \leq c 2^{-\frac{3}{2} j_{0}} \leq c 2^{-j_{1} / 2}
\end{aligned}
$$

with $c$ independent of $f \in \mathcal{F}(B)$. The overall reconstruction

$$
\hat{f}=\sum_{k} \beta_{j_{0}, k} \varphi_{j_{0}, k}+\sum_{j=j_{0}}^{j_{1}-1} \hat{\alpha}_{j, k} \psi_{j, k}
$$

has error

$$
\begin{aligned}
\|f-\hat{f}\|_{2} & \leq\left\|f-P_{j_{1}} f\right\|_{2}+\left\|P_{j_{1}} f-\hat{f}\right\|_{2} \\
& =\left\|f-P_{j_{1}} f\right\|_{2}+\|\hat{\theta}-\theta\|_{2} \\
& \leq c 2^{-j_{1} / 2}
\end{aligned}
$$

again with $c$ independent of $f \in \mathcal{F}(B)$. This is of the same order of magnitude as the error of linear sampling.

The compressed sensing scheme takes a total of $2^{j_{0}}$ samples of resumé coefficients and $n \leq c 2^{j_{0}} \log \left(2^{j_{1}}\right)$ samples associated with detail coefficients, for a total $\leq c \cdot j_{1} \cdot 2^{j_{1} / 3}$ pieces of information. It achieves error comparable to classical sampling based on $2^{j_{1}}$ samples. Thus, it needs dramatically fewer samples for comparable accuracy: roughly speaking, only the cube root of the number of samples of linear sampling.

To achieve this dramatic reduction in sampling, we need an information operator based on some $\Phi$ satisfying CS1-CS3. The underlying measurement kernels will be of the form


\begin{equation*}
\xi_{i}=\sum_{j=1}^{m} \Phi_{i, \ell} \phi_{\ell}, \quad i=1, \ldots, n \tag{VI.1}
\end{equation*}


where the collection $\left(\phi_{\ell}\right)_{\ell=1}^{m}$ is simply an enumeration of the wavelets $\psi_{j, k}$, with $j_{0} \leq j<j_{1}$ and $0 \leq k<2^{j}$.

Definition 7.1: Let $|J|=k$. We say that $\Phi_{J}$ offers an $\epsilon$-isometry between $\ell_{2}(J)$ and $\ell_{1}^{n}$ if $\forall \alpha \in \boldsymbol{R}^{k}$


\begin{equation*}
(1-\epsilon) \cdot\|\alpha\|_{2} \leq \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2 n}} \cdot\left\|\Phi_{J} \alpha\right\|_{1} \leq(1+\epsilon) \cdot\|\alpha\|_{2} . \tag{VII.1}
\end{equation*}

To prove this, we first need a lemma about individual subsets $J$ proven in [11].

Lemma 7.5: Fix $\epsilon>0$. Choose $\delta$ so that


\begin{equation*}
(1-3 \delta)(1-\delta)^{-1} \geq(1-\epsilon)^{1 / 2} \tag{VII.2}
\end{equation*}


and


\begin{equation*}
(1+\delta)(1-\delta)^{-1} \leq(1+\epsilon)^{1 / 2} \tag{VII.3}
\end{equation*}


Choose $\rho_{1}=\rho_{1}(\epsilon)$ so that

$$
\rho_{1} \cdot(1+2 / \delta)<\delta^{2} \frac{2}{\pi}
$$

and let $\beta(\epsilon)$ denote the difference between the two sides. For a subset $J$ in $\{1, \ldots, m\}$, let $\Omega_{n, J}$ denote the event that $\Phi_{J}$ furnishes an $\epsilon$-isometry to $\ell_{1}^{n}$. Then as $n \rightarrow \infty$

$$
\max _{|J| \leq \rho_{1} n} P\left(\Omega_{n, J}^{c}\right) \leq 2 \exp (-\beta(\epsilon) n(1+o(1))) .
$$

Now note that the event of interest for Lemma 7.4 is

$$
\Omega_{n}^{2}=\cap_{|J| \leq \rho n / \log (m)} \Omega_{n, J} ;
$$

to finish, apply the individual Lemma 7.5 together with the combining principle in Lemma 7.3.

