Consider the convex optimization problem


\begin{equation*}
\min \left\|\beta_{J^{c}}\right\|_{1} \text { subject to } \Phi_{J^{c}} \beta_{J^{c}}=-v . \tag{VII.8}
\end{equation*}


This can be written as a linear program, by the same sort of construction as given for (IV.1). By the duality theorem for linear programming, the value of the primal program is at least the value of the dual

$$
\max \langle v, y\rangle \text { subject to }\left|\left\langle\phi_{i}, y\right\rangle\right| \leq 1, \quad i \in J^{c}
$$

Lemma 7.6 gives us a supply of dual-feasible vectors and hence a lower bound on the dual program. Take $\sigma=\operatorname{sgn}(v)$; we can find $y$ which is dual-feasible and obeys

$$
\begin{aligned}
\langle v, y\rangle & \geq\left\langle v, \epsilon_{n} \sigma\right\rangle-\left\|y-\epsilon_{n} \sigma\right\|_{2}\|v\|_{2} \\
& \geq \epsilon_{n}\|v\|_{1}-\epsilon_{n} \delta\|\sigma\|_{2}\|v\|_{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

picking $\delta$ appropriately and taking into account the spherical sections theorem, for sufficiently large $n$, we have $\delta\|\sigma\|_{2}\|v\|_{2} \leq \frac{1}{4}\|v\|_{1}$; (VII.7) follows with $\eta_{3}=3 / 4$.

