Lemma 7.7 will be proven in a section of its own. We now show that it implies Lemma 7.6.

We recall a standard implication of so-called VapnikCervonenkis theory [46]

$$
\# \Sigma_{J} \leq\binom{ n}{0}+\binom{n}{1}+\cdots+\binom{n}{|J|} .
$$

Notice that if $\|J\|<\rho n / \log (m)$, then

$$
\log \left(\# \Sigma_{J}\right) \leq \rho \cdot n+\log (n)
$$

while also

$$
\log \#\{J:|J|<\rho n / \log (m), J \subset\{1, \ldots, m\}\} \leq \rho n .
$$

Hence, the total number of sign patterns generated by operators $\Phi_{J}$ obeys

$$
\log \#\left\{\sigma: \sigma \in \Sigma_{J},|J|<\rho n / \log (m)\right\} \leq n \cdot 2 \rho+\log (n) .
$$

Now $\beta$ furnished by Lemma 7.7 is positive, so pick $\rho_{3}<\beta / 2$ with $\rho_{3}>0$. Define

$$
\Omega_{n}^{3}=\cap_{|J|<\rho_{3} n / \log (m)} \cap_{\sigma \in \Sigma_{J}} \Omega_{\sigma, J}
$$

where $\Omega_{\sigma, J}$ denotes the instance of the event (called $\Omega_{\sigma}$ in the statement of Lemma 7.7) generated by a specific $\sigma, J$ combination. On the event $\Omega_{n}^{3}$, every sign pattern associated with any $\Phi_{J}$ obeying $|J|<\rho_{3} n / \log (m)$ is almost dual feasible. Now

$$
\begin{aligned}
P\left(\left(\Omega_{n}^{3}\right)^{c}\right) \leq & \#\left\{\sigma: \sigma \in \Sigma_{J},|J|<\rho_{3} n / \log (m)\right\} \\
& \times \exp (-n \beta) \\
\leq & \exp \left\{-n\left(\beta-2 \rho_{3}\right)+\log (n)\right\}
\end{aligned}
$$

which tends to zero exponentially rapidly. \(\square\)

